From c9824a9368fa2fa245042c8444f3220b41117a3b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philip Mueller <claude@philipmueller.ch>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 06:33:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 57/57] fix(grpo): keep masked rows out of the group baseline

grpo_train and async_grpo_train call compute_advantage without valid_mask,
so the group-relative estimators fall back to an all-ones mask. A row that
is left out of the loss (mask_sample from the environment, overlong
filtering, sequence-level logprob error masking) therefore still enters
its group's baseline and std with its reward, usually 0. With NeMo Gym,
rollouts that do not finish (agent timeout, infrastructure failure) are
returned with mask_sample set and reward 0, so every such rollout pulls
its siblings' leave-one-out baseline down and raises their advantages,
even though the rollout itself is not trained on.

The SingleController path already passes final_sample_mask as valid_mask,
and the GRPO, GDPO and Reinforce++ estimators honor it. Pass the training
sample mask (sample_mask after all filters) as valid_mask on both driver
paths as well.

Behaviour:
- No masked row: the mask is all ones, which the estimators treat exactly
  like no mask, so advantages are bit-identical.
- A group keeps its other rows; their baseline and std come from the
  unmasked rows only, as if the masked rollouts had never been sampled.
- A group with one or zero unmasked rows gets advantage 0 on every row
  (calculate_baseline_and_std_per_prompt sets baseline = reward), so it
  adds no policy-gradient signal; no NaN.

Tests: the estimator matches a run on the unmasked rows only (with the
default leave-one-out baseline and std normalization), groups with one or
no valid rows get zero advantage, and an all-ones mask equals no mask;
grpo_train and async_grpo_train pass the sample mask as valid_mask, with
and without a masked row.

Signed-off-by: Philip Mueller <claude@philipmueller.ch>
